<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sumuabum</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. c. 1894–1881 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century-bce">19th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sumuabum was an Amorite chieftain who founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-babylonian-empire">First Dynasty of Babylon</a> around 1894 BCE. According to the Babylonian Empire profile, he declared independence and began building a defensive wall around the city. He was the first of the rulers who built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> into a regional power.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Sumu-abum, also written Sūmû-abum, Su-abu or Sūmabum, was an Amorite and is counted as the first king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-babylonian-empire">First Dynasty of Babylon</a>, also called the Amorite Dynasty. His reign is dated c. 1894–1881 BCE in the middle chronology. It fell in the period after the collapse of the Ur III Empire, when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/city-state">city-states</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isin">Isin</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/larsa">Larsa</a> were the major powers of the region. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> was then a minor administrative centre in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>.</p><p>Sumu-abum freed a small area of land that had been ruled by the fellow Amorite city-state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazallu">Kazallu</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> lay within it. Neither he nor the three Amorite kings who followed him claims the title King of Babylon, which suggests that the town was still of little importance at the time.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>About fourteen year names of Sumu-abum are known. They record the building of the great city wall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a>, the temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ninisina">Ninisina</a>, the temple Emah of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanna">Nanna</a> and the wall of Dilbat, a city 25 kilometres south of Babylon that he controlled. In his third year he claimed to have conquered Elip, a major city of the Manana Dynasty, and another year name says he destroyed or seized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazallu">Kazallu</a>.</p><p>By his tenth year he had gained control of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kish">Kish</a>, 12 kilometres east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a>, which the Manana dynasty had held for some time. Kish was briefly lost to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/larsa">Larsa</a> and recovered in his thirteenth year. It was no longer a powerful city but kept an outsized symbolic importance, and one year name says that he gave it a city wall reaching heaven. He is also known to have become king of Kisurra, although an alternative view holds that the Sumu-abum who controlled Kisurra was a ruler of the 6th Dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruk">Uruk</a>, before Sîn-kāšid.</p><p>The Old Babylonian levels at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> lie below the water table and have not been excavated, so what is known of him comes from other sites: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazallu">Kazallu</a>, Dilbat, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sippar">Sippar</a> and Kisurra. He should not be confused with Isi-sümü-abum of the same period, who ruled a city not yet identified. A tablet from Sippar, BM 80328, lists rulers of Babylon and names fourteen unknown rulers before Sūmû-abum.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sumu-abum” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Around 1894 BCE an Amorite chieftain named <b>Sumu-abum</b> (Sumuabum) took control of Babylon, traditionally from the neighbouring city-state of Kazallu, and began building a defensive wall around the city.</blockquote><a href="/empire/babylonian-empire">Babylonian Empire · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
